// FIXED-POINT ARITHMETIC, IN THE CORPUS BECAUSE THE HAND-WRITTEN DIFFERENTIAL
// CANNOT DRIVE IT.
//
// `Op::Add`, `Op::Sub` and `Op::Neg` lower for a matched `Fixed` pair. Verifying
// that needs a function TAKING `Fixed` parameters: an operand's width is trusted
// only where a signature states it and the chunk never writes the local, so a
// `let x = w as Fixed<16>` is `Unknown` and refuses. The entry point cannot take
// `Fixed`, because the harness and the virtual machine both pass integers.
//
// So the probe is necessarily MULTI-FUNCTION, and the hand-written differential
// lowers `chunks[0]` while running the ENTRY POINT in the virtual machine --
// different functions whenever a program has more than one. That harness now
// refuses a multi-function source rather than comparing two unrelated things;
// this file is the route that remains.
//
// A FALSE PASS IS WHAT EXPOSED ALL OF THIS. A two-function test compared
// `add_fx` natively against `main` in the virtual machine and PASSED, because
// fixed-point scaling is linear and `((a<<16) + (b<<16)) >> 16` equals `a + b`.
// Agreement by mathematical accident, between two functions that were never the
// same one.
//
// The corpus differential drives whole modules through the entry point on both
// sides, so it compares like with like.
//
// WITNESSES: Add, Sub, Neg, GetField, GetIndex, NewComposite
//
// THE LINE ABOVE IS A MACHINE-CHECKED CLAIM, not a comment.
// `witness_integrity.rs` compiles this file and asserts every opcode named
// there is actually emitted. A witness that silently STOPS witnessing --
// because the reference compiler folded the construct away, or because a
// function moved to another file -- would otherwise drop a census figure
// with nothing going red.
//
// That is not hypothetical: the `Op::IsStruct` construct was repaired out of
// existence by the `v0.2.3` line one hour after this corpus adopted it.

fn add_fx(x: Fixed<16>, y: Fixed<16>) -> Fixed<16> {
  x + y
}

fn sub_fx(x: Fixed<16>, y: Fixed<16>) -> Fixed<16> {
  x - y
}

fn neg_fx(x: Fixed<16>) -> Fixed<16> {
  -x
}

// A COMPOSITE CARRYING `Fixed` FIELDS, and it was ABSENT for one increment
// because it found a gap by trying.
//
// The operand-width widening made such a composite PACK -- the reference sizes
// `struct { a: Fixed<16>, b: Fixed<16> }` at sixteen bytes, identical to a pair
// of `Word`s. But READING one back refused:
//
//   pair_sum: UnsupportedOp("GetField reading Fixed is not lowered")
//
// Packing a field and reading it are SEPARATE ARMS, and only packing followed
// from the width. `GetField` and `GetIndex` now read a `Fixed` exactly as they
// read an `Int`: eight raw bytes of Q-format bits, no zero-extension, no mask,
// no rescale. The bits are the value.
//
// IT IS HERE RATHER THAN IN A HAND-WRITTEN TEST because one refusing chunk
// exempts a whole MODULE, and because the hand-written differential lowers
// `chunks[0]` while running the ENTRY POINT -- so it cannot drive anything
// multi-function, and a `Fixed` parameter requires a non-entry function.

struct Pair { a: Fixed<16>, b: Fixed<16> }

fn pair_sum(x: Fixed<16>) -> Fixed<16> {
  let p = Pair { a: x, b: x };
  add_fx(p.a, p.b)
}

// An ARRAY of `Fixed`, so the element read is exercised as well as the field
// read. The two are different arms and the same widening reached both.
fn elem_sum(x: Fixed<16>) -> Fixed<16> {
  let xs = [x, x];
  add_fx(xs[0], xs[1])
}

fn main(seed: Word) -> Word {
  let x = seed as Fixed<16>;
  let y = (seed + 3) as Fixed<16>;
  let s = add_fx(x, y);
  let d = sub_fx(x, y);
  let n = neg_fx(x);
  let p = pair_sum(x);
  let e = elem_sum(y);
  (s as Word) + (d as Word) + (n as Word) + (p as Word) + (e as Word)
}
